## 4. Commonware Component Mapping

Commonware supplies independent primitives rather than a prescribed blockchain runtime. The project will use the following components directly.

| System concern              | Commonware component                                     | v1 use                                                          |
| --------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Production execution        | `commonware_runtime::tokio`                              | Live node runtime                                               |
| Deterministic execution     | `commonware_runtime::deterministic`                      | Simulation, schedule replay, fault testing                      |
| Consensus                   | `commonware_consensus::simplex`                          | BFT block agreement                                             |
| Application integration     | `commonware_consensus::Application`                      | Application block proposal and verification boundary            |
| Ordered finalization        | `commonware_consensus::marshal::standard::Deferred`      | Finalized block ordering and ancestry recovery                  |
| Stateful execution          | `commonware_glue::stateful::Stateful`                    | Speculative fork state, verification, finalization, recovery    |
| Block distribution          | `commonware_broadcast::buffered`                         | Block dissemination and cache                                   |
| Missing-data recovery       | `commonware_resolver::TargetedResolver`                  | Targeted block and state resolution                             |
| Live peer network           | `commonware_p2p::authenticated::lookup`                  | Fixed authenticated committee with known addresses              |
| Simulated peer network      | `commonware_p2p::simulated`                              | Drops, delays, partitions, and deterministic network faults     |
| Consensus signatures        | `commonware_consensus::simplex::scheme::ed25519::Scheme` | Attributable consensus certificates without DKG                 |
| Operator signatures         | `commonware_cryptography::ed25519`                       | Signed customer and validation actions                          |
| Hashing                     | `commonware_cryptography::sha256`                        | IDs, content references, commitments, roots                     |
| Canonical binary format     | `commonware_codec`                                       | Consensus-safe action, block, event, and state encoding         |
| Current authenticated state | `commonware_storage::qmdb::current`                      | Merkleized application state                                    |
| Finalized archives          | `commonware_storage::archive::immutable`                 | Finalized blocks and certificates                               |
| Append-only records         | `commonware_storage::journal`                            | Receipts, operator traces, and local recovery records           |
| Actor structure             | `commonware_actor`                                       | Mempool, ingress, finalization, and operator-facing node actors |
| Encoding compatibility      | `commonware_conformance`                                 | Locked codec vectors and hashes                                 |
| Stateful fuzzing            | `commonware_invariants::minifuzz`                        | Transition and mechanism invariant exploration                  |

Commonware’s runtime exposes deterministic and Tokio-backed execution under compatible runtime abstractions, which is the main reason the live node and simulator can share component implementations.

Simplex accepts application-defined blocks and separates agreement from application execution, broadcast, and synchronization. The marshal layer supplies ordered, at-least-once finalized delivery and block backfilling.

The authenticated P2P implementation supports a fixed authenticated peer set. The simulated implementation supports controlled network behavior for deterministic tests.

`commonware_glue::stateful` is specifically intended to maintain speculative state for pending consensus forks, replay missing ancestry, commit the winning state at finalization, and prune losing branches. It can wrap `marshal::standard::Deferred`, which is the required integration for this project.

Alto is the primary integration reference for wiring buffered broadcast, Simplex, marshal, resolver, runtime, and immutable storage. It is a reference, not a codebase to fork wholesale.

