## 29. Deterministic Laboratory

The laboratory uses the real:

* canonical types;
* codecs;
* transition engine;
* mechanism registry;
* block model;
* Commonware deterministic runtime;
* simulated P2P implementation.

It must not implement a second toy version of the economy.

### 29.1 Laboratory modes

#### Scripted mode

Pure Rust operator policies produce actions deterministically.

Use for:

* smoke tests;
* mechanism controls;
* known exploit regressions;
* invariant tests;
* exact CI replay.

#### Trace-replay mode

Previously captured operator decisions are replayed without invoking a model.

Use for:

* reproducing economic exploits;
* comparing mechanism versions against identical decisions where causal assumptions permit;
* network-fault and scheduling variations;
* dependency-upgrade regression.

#### Closed-loop agent mode

External agents receive observations and return decisions during the run.

Use for:

* emergent strategy search;
* novel exploit discovery;
* realistic autonomous validation.

Model generation itself is not deterministic. Therefore closed-loop runs are reproducible at the level of:

* exact observations;
* exact prompts;
* exact model/provider configuration;
* captured raw responses;
* parsed decisions;
* signed actions;
* resulting network execution.

A replay uses the captured actions rather than recalling the model.

### 29.2 Deterministic boundary

Remote model calls MUST NOT occur inside Commonware deterministic actors.

The laboratory pauses at operator decision points:

```text
deterministic network reaches decision boundary
→ observation snapshot written
→ external operator host invoked
→ decision and raw output captured
→ decision converted to signed action
→ deterministic execution resumes
```

This prevents nondeterministic model behavior from contaminating consensus replay.

