## 44. Core Invariants

### Ledger invariants

* Every accepted action has a valid signature.
* Actor nonces are contiguous and monotonic.
* An object ID matches its canonical content.
* A job’s artifact and claims never change.
* A job revision creates a new job ID.
* Every attestation references an existing job and claim.
* Every evidence reference has exactly one registered producer.
* Every resolution satisfies its configured resolution policy.
* Every block’s roots reproduce exactly from its actions and parent state.
* Replaying finalized blocks produces the same state root.

### Role invariants

* Consensus keys and network actor keys are distinct types.
* Consensus participation never changes validation reputation.
* Validation reputation never changes consensus committee membership.
* Resolution-authority actions never earn validation reputation.

### Mechanism invariants

* A mechanism writes only to its namespace.
* Mechanisms cannot modify semantic verdicts.
* Reputation is non-transferable.
* Customer standing, when introduced, cannot alter resolution truth.
* No module can rewrite prior canonical events.
* The mechanism set is constant after genesis.
* Module dependency graph is acyclic.
* Mechanism arithmetic is checked and deterministic.

### Experimental invariants

* Hidden truth is inaccessible to operators before resolution.
* Operator observations match the declared information policy.
* Resource use is captured.
* Formal thresholds precede formal outcomes.
* Calibration fixtures do not enter held-out evaluation.
* Every reported run has a reproducible protocol/action trace.

---

## 45. Security Requirements

### Consensus-path restrictions

The consensus path MUST NOT:

* fetch remote URLs;
* invoke Git;
* execute tests;
* call model APIs;
* parse arbitrary Markdown;
* run shell commands;
* use floating point;
* use unbounded allocations;
* rely on locale or system timezone;
* read mutable environment configuration after startup.

### Decode hardening

Every decoder needs:

* maximum allocation limits;
* malformed-input tests;
* truncation tests;
* invalid-enum tests;
* oversized-vector tests;
* duplicate-key tests where applicable.

### Signature hardening

Signed actions include:

* chain ID;
* protocol version;
* actor key;
* nonce;
* expiry;
* canonical payload.

This prevents cross-chain and cross-version replay.

### Evidence privacy

Evidence may contain private code, vulnerability details, credentials, or customer data.

The chain stores hashes and locator hints only.

The operator and consumer layers must allow private evidence access controls later without changing canonical evidence identity.

### RPC isolation

The node RPC must not expose:

* consensus private keys;
* operator private keys;
* hidden experiment labels;
* unredacted operator prompts by default;
* evidence bodies without explicit authorization.

